<h2>Why Supplements Are Common in Bexar County</h2>
<p>San Antonio roofs have taken repeated hail over the years. NOAA has recorded more than 600 hail reports in Bexar County since 1957, with the busiest months in April and May. Repeated storms, aging decking, and older roofs that have been overlaid can all hide problems that only appear during tear-off. Older homes in historic neighborhoods such as Monte Vista and King William can have plank decking, plaster walls, and other materials that complicate repairs.</p>
<p>Freeze losses are another frequent source. During Winter Storm Uri in February 2021, many San Antonio homes had pipes burst in walls and attics, and the full extent of wet materials often was not known until demolition and drying were underway. Water can travel under slab-on-grade flooring and behind cabinets in ways a first inspection cannot see.</p>
<p>Code requirements also matter. Re-roofing and most structural repairs in the City of San Antonio generally require a permit through the Development Services Department, and inspectors may require items that were not in the original estimate. Whether the policy pays for them depends on ordinance or law coverage. See our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page.</p>

