<h2>How to Document a Supplement</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stop and notify.</strong> When hidden damage is found, tell the insurer promptly in writing, and ask whether it wants to re-inspect before the area is closed up.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph before, during, and after.</strong> Take wide shots for location and close-ups for detail, with a tape measure where it helps.</li>
<li><strong>Measure.</strong> Record quantities: sheets of decking, linear feet of framing, square feet of drywall.</li>
<li><strong>Explain the connection.</strong> Show how each item relates to the covered loss.</li>
<li><strong>Itemize.</strong> Present the additions in a line-item format the insurer can compare with its estimate, ideally in Xactimate.</li>
<li><strong>Attach support.</strong> Include invoices, permit requirements, manufacturer specifications, or inspector notes.</li>
<li><strong>Follow up.</strong> Track the response and keep copies of everything.</li>
</ol>
<p>The <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> covers the photo and record-keeping basics.</p>

<h2>What Makes a Supplement Easy to Review</h2>
<p>Insurance adjusters review a lot of supplements, and the ones that move quickly tend to share a few traits. They are organized by room or roof slope rather than as a pile of photos. Each requested item points to a specific photo and measurement. They explain why the item is needed in a sentence or two, for example that decking was found delaminated and soft on the north slope during tear-off, with eight sheets replaced. They separate items that are clearly storm-related from items that are code or permit requirements, because those may fall under different coverage. And they avoid padding: a supplement that includes questionable items invites scrutiny of everything else on the list.</p>
<p>Timing matters too. A supplement sent while the roof is still open, with a chance for the insurer to look, is usually easier to support than one sent months after the work is finished. Keep your contractor's crew informed so they photograph problems before covering them up.</p>

<h2>How a San Antonio Public Adjuster Helps With Supplements</h2>
<p>As a San Antonio Public Adjuster, we review the insurer's original estimate, inspect the property, coordinate with your contractor to document what is found during repairs, and prepare a supplement in Xactimate with supporting photos and explanations. We then present it to the insurer and follow up until it is addressed. If the original settlement was significantly short across the board, the issue may be broader than a supplement; see <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> and <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a>.</p>
<p>Under Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102, a public adjuster may not take part in the repair of a property on a claim they adjust, so your contractor remains your choice. We cannot guarantee that a supplement will be approved. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Supplements vs. Disputes</h2>
<p>A supplement is part of the normal claim process: new information, revised estimate. If the insurer reviews a supplement and still disagrees about the amount, <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> may be an option. If it denies an item as not covered, that is a coverage question, and a Texas attorney can advise on legal options. Public adjusters do not give legal advice.</p>

